skip
MEASUREMENT HARNESS, no graded answer. It captures three screenshots at named beats and
asserts nothing about any of them. The three things its header says it "verifies" -- that
Test.Screenshot returns a path, that the PNGs reach the per-run screenshot directory, and
that the verdict JSON's screenshots[] lists all three -- are all read out of the run
directory by a human. The Lua evaluates no predicate.

WHY SKIP AND NOT PASS. Until 2026-09-01 the terminal verdict was `Test.Pass`, so this
scenario contributed a green to every `run-batch.sh --all` tally whether or not a single
PNG landed. `Test.Skip` is the harness's own word for "ran, produced no graded answer"
(TestGlobal.cs) and run-batch.sh maps it to the SKIP bucket. The scenario is not hidden
and still runs; the verdict just stops claiming an answer it never computed.

REMOVE THIS FILE in the same commit as whatever gives the scenario a real assertion --
asserting on the screenshots[] array length would be the obvious one and would make this
a genuine pipeline guard. A PASS against a live `skip` declaration grades STALE/RED on
purpose: a scenario that STARTS grading shows up loudly instead of silently changing
meaning.

NOT EXPOSED TO expected-status.sh's KNOWN GAP. That gap is specific to `fail`
declarations, where a watchdog TIMEOUT-FAIL also exits 1 and therefore grades green.
A timeout reaches expected_status_grade as FAIL, and declared-`skip` + FAIL is RED.
